{"issue":{"title":"Add a jsonl-lint tool that reports every invalid JSONL row by line number","problem":"The repo has no tools at all: there is no `packages/` directory, README.md says \"No tools yet: `packages/` is empty\", and root `npm test` just prints 'no workspaces under packages/'. There is nothing a person or the harness can point at a JSONL journal to find rows that are not valid JSON (torn writes, hand edits); today such rows are only discovered when a reader breaks on them.","reason":"Journals are kept as JSONL, and a single torn or hand-edited row breaks every reader. A standalone linter that names each bad row by line number and fails with a non-zero exit lets people and scripts find and fix the damage before a reader trips over it.","outcome":"A new npm workspace `packages/jsonl-lint` (Node 22+, plain ES modules, no build step, no runtime dependencies) provides a `jsonl-lint` command. Given a file path, it prints one report line per row that is not valid JSON, naming its 1-based line number, and exits 0 only when every row is valid; a missing file or no argument is an error with a non-zero exit. It has node:test tests run by root `npm test`, and the README lists it.","expect":["After `npm ci` at the repo root, `node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint` exists and runs the tool, and `packages/jsonl-lint/package.json` declares no runtime `dependencies` (absent or empty).","For a file whose rows are `{\"a\":1}`, `{\"a\":`, `[1,2]`, `{a:1}`, `42` (each followed by a newline), `jsonl-lint bad.jsonl` exits non-zero and its combined stdout+stderr contains exactly two lines starting with `bad.jsonl:<n>:`, namely `bad.jsonl:2:` and `bad.jsonl:4:` in that order; rows 1, 3 and 5 (an object, an array and a bare number) are not reported.","A file whose last row is torn and has no trailing newline (`{\"a\":1}` newline `{\"b\"`) makes `jsonl-lint` exit non-zero and report exactly one row, `torn.jsonl:2:`.","For a file in which every row is valid JSON and the file ends with a newline, `jsonl-lint good.jsonl` exits 0 and prints no line starting with `good.jsonl:`; the trailing newline is not reported as an empty row. An empty (0-byte) file also exits 0.","`jsonl-lint missing.jsonl` for a path that does not exist exits non-zero, its combined output contains `missing.jsonl`, and the output contains no Node stack-trace frame (no line matching `^\\s+at `).","Running `jsonl-lint` with no file argument exits non-zero and prints a usage or error message rather than a stack trace (no line matching `^\\s+at `).","Root `npm test` exits 0 and runs a node:test suite for the jsonl-lint workspace that reports at least one passing test.","README.md no longer says there are no tools / that `packages/` is empty, and lists `jsonl-lint` with how to run it."],"proofs":[{"line":"After `npm ci` at the repo root, `node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint` exists and runs the tool, and `packages/jsonl-lint/package.json` declares no runtime `dependencies` (absent or empty).","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root: `npm ci && test -x node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint && echo BIN_OK; node -e \"const p=require('./packages/jsonl-lint/package.json'); const d=p.dependencies||{}; process.exit(Object.keys(d).length?1:0)\" && echo NODEPS_OK`. Both BIN_OK and NODEPS_OK must print."},{"line":"For a file whose rows are `{\"a\":1}`, `{\"a\":`, `[1,2]`, `{a:1}`, `42` (each followed by a newline), `jsonl-lint bad.jsonl` exits non-zero and its combined stdout+stderr contains exactly two lines starting with `bad.jsonl:<n>:`, namely `bad.jsonl:2:` and `bad.jsonl:4:` in that order; rows 1, 3 and 5 (an object, an array and a bare number) are not reported.","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root after `npm ci`: `R=$PWD; cd \"$(mktemp -d)\"; printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"a\":\\n[1,2]\\n{a:1}\\n42\\n' > bad.jsonl; out=$(\"$R/node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint\" bad.jsonl 2>&1); echo \"exit=$?\"; echo \"$out\" | grep -oE '^bad\\.jsonl:[0-9]+:'`. Expect exit non-zero and the grep output to be exactly `bad.jsonl:2:` then `bad.jsonl:4:`."},{"line":"A file whose last row is torn and has no trailing newline (`{\"a\":1}` newline `{\"b\"`) makes `jsonl-lint` exit non-zero and report exactly one row, `torn.jsonl:2:`.","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root after `npm ci`: `R=$PWD; cd \"$(mktemp -d)\"; printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\"' > torn.jsonl; out=$(\"$R/node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint\" torn.jsonl 2>&1); echo \"exit=$?\"; echo \"$out\" | grep -oE '^torn\\.jsonl:[0-9]+:'`. Expect exit non-zero and grep output exactly `torn.jsonl:2:`."},{"line":"For a file in which every row is valid JSON and the file ends with a newline, `jsonl-lint good.jsonl` exits 0 and prints no line starting with `good.jsonl:`; the trailing newline is not reported as an empty row. An empty (0-byte) file also exits 0.","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root after `npm ci`: `R=$PWD; cd \"$(mktemp -d)\"; printf '{\"a\":1}\\n[1,2]\\n\"x\"\\nnull\\n' > good.jsonl; out=$(\"$R/node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint\" good.jsonl 2>&1); echo \"exit=$?\"; echo \"$out\" | grep -c '^good\\.jsonl:'; : > empty.jsonl; \"$R/node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint\" empty.jsonl; echo \"empty_exit=$?\"`. Expect exit=0, count 0, empty_exit=0."},{"line":"`jsonl-lint missing.jsonl` for a path that does not exist exits non-zero, its combined output contains `missing.jsonl`, and the output contains no Node stack-trace frame (no line matching `^\\s+at `).","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root after `npm ci`: `R=$PWD; cd \"$(mktemp -d)\"; out=$(\"$R/node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint\" missing.jsonl 2>&1); echo \"exit=$?\"; echo \"$out\" | grep -c 'missing\\.jsonl'; echo \"$out\" | grep -cE '^\\s+at '`. Expect exit non-zero, first count ≥1, second count 0."},{"line":"Running `jsonl-lint` with no file argument exits non-zero and prints a usage or error message rather than a stack trace (no line matching `^\\s+at `).","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root after `npm ci`: `out=$(node_modules/.bin/jsonl-lint 2>&1); echo \"exit=$?\"; echo \"$out\"; echo \"$out\" | grep -cE '^\\s+at '`. Expect exit non-zero, non-empty message, count 0."},{"line":"Root `npm test` exits 0 and runs a node:test suite for the jsonl-lint workspace that reports at least one passing test.","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root: `npm ci && npm test 2>&1 | tee /tmp/jl-test.log; echo \"exit=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"; grep -E '^> .*jsonl-lint.* test' /tmp/jl-test.log; grep -E '^# pass [1-9]' /tmp/jl-test.log`. Expect exit=0, the jsonl-lint workspace test script line present, and a `# pass N` line with N ≥ 1 (also confirmed by the `test` CI check on the PR)."},{"line":"README.md no longer says there are no tools / that `packages/` is empty, and lists `jsonl-lint` with how to run it.","proof":"read","how":"Read README.md at the PR head: the 'What is here' section must not claim `packages/` is empty or that there are no tools, and must mention `jsonl-lint` and how to invoke it on a file."}],"non_goals":["Schema validation of rows (required keys, types); only JSON syntax is checked.","Repairing, rewriting or truncating invalid rows.","Reading from stdin or linting several files / directories in one call.","Publishing the package to npm or wiring it into the ccp_machine harness.","Performance tuning for very large files beyond reading the file normally."],"assumptions":["The tool lives at `packages/jsonl-lint` as an npm workspace with a `bin` named `jsonl-lint`, per CLAUDE.md's one-folder-per-tool rule; package-lock.json is updated so `npm ci` still succeeds.","Report format, which the request left open: one line per invalid row, in file order, starting with `<path as given>:<1-based line number>:` (grep/compiler style), optionally followed by the parse error. Stdout vs stderr is left to the builder; checks read both.","Any JSON value (object, array, string, number, true/false/null) counts as a valid row, not only objects.","A single trailing newline at the end of the file does not create an extra empty row. Blank or whitespace-only lines in the middle of a file are left to the builder (no acceptance line depends on them).","Exit codes: 0 when all rows are valid, non-zero otherwise. Suggested: 1 for invalid rows, 2 for usage errors or an unreadable/missing file, but only 'non-zero' is required.","Lines ending in CRLF are treated as valid when the content before `\\r` is valid JSON, because JSON allows trailing whitespace."]}}